      "answer": "# CSV Parser\n\n```python\nimport csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Union\n\n\ndef parse_csv(\n    file_path: Union[str, Path],\n    *,\n    delimiter: str = \",\",\n    encoding: str = \"utf-8\",\n) -> List[Dict[str, str]]:\n    \"\"\"Parse a CSV file into a list of dictionaries.\n\n    Each row in the CSV is converted into a dictionary keyed by the\n    column headers from the first row. All values are returned as\n    strings; callers are responsible for any type conversion.\n\n    Args:\n        file_path: Path to the CSV file to parse.\n        delimiter: Field delimiter used in the CSV file. Defaults to \",\".\n        encoding: Text encoding used to read the file. Defaults to \"utf-8\".\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of dictionaries, one per data row, mapping column names\n        to their string values.\n\n    Raises:\n        FileNotFoundError: If the file does not exist.\n        csv.Error: If the file is malformed (e.g., inconsistent columns).\n\n    Example:\n        >>> rows = parse_csv(\"data.csv\")\n        >>> rows[0][\"name\"]\n        'Alice",
